From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] SystemUI: the image wallpaper is drawn only when it is shown

ImageWallpaper draws itself whenever its surface is created or changes and
whenever the wallpaper offsets change, shown or not. On PW3 no window shows
the wallpaper (Launcher3 and Recents are opaque white, device overlay), and
no app on the device can set one (nothing handles SET_WALLPAPER): the
built-in white picture was drawn into a buffer the size of the screen
(6,063.5 KiB, 1072x1448 RGBA) that SurfaceFlinger then kept for good, and
drawn again whenever the home screen changed page (Test V40.1).

It is now drawn only while the engine reports it visible (Engine.isVisible(),
the last visibility WallpaperService reported): from onVisibilityChanged(true)
once a window shows the wallpaper (the lock screen does, when the user sets
one up), and from the other callbacks while it is shown. WallpaperService
itself calls onVisibilityChanged(true) and (false) around a new surface while
the wallpaper is hidden; that toggle does not count as a showing, and
mVisible starts false so that the first real showing still draws. Until then
the surface gets no buffer. WindowManager's wait for the wallpaper at boot
counts the window drawn once the engine reports finishDrawing, which
WallpaperService.updateSurface() does after the surface callbacks whether or
not they drew.

--- a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/ImageWallpaper.java
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/ImageWallpaper.java
@@ -112,7 +112,9 @@
         private Display mDefaultDisplay;
         private final DisplayInfo mTmpDisplayInfo = new DisplayInfo();
 
-        boolean mVisible = true;
+        // PW3: false at first, so that the first time the wallpaper is shown is a change that
+        // draws it (onVisibilityChanged()); see drawFrame().
+        boolean mVisible = false;
         boolean mOffsetsChanged;
         int mLastXTranslation;
         int mLastYTranslation;
@@ -309,7 +311,10 @@
         }
 
         void drawFrame() {
-            if (!mSurfaceValid) {
+            // PW3: a wallpaper that no window shows is not drawn, so that its buffer, the size
+            // of the screen, is not allocated. isVisible() is what the engine last reported;
+            // the visibility toggle it sends when the surface is created is not a showing.
+            if (!mSurfaceValid || !isVisible()) {
                 return;
             }
             try {
